{
  "body": "{\"model\": \"nomic-embed-text\", \"input\": [\"A derivation is a value the app computes from what it reads: facts, tables, files, or another derivation. It is declared with `derive`, and the app recomputes it when something it reads moves. Nothing calls it; a view or a handler names it, and the value is there.\",\"Who holds a derivation is inferred from what it reads. A derivation that reads only external sources is the app's, shared by every reader. One that reads a session's facts is the session's, computed for that reader alone.\",\"A derivation over an external source stays fresh by its window: `fresh = 300` recomputes it at most every five minutes, and the default of zero means as fresh as the loop, recomputed every render.\",\"A write reaches every reader. When a handler writes a table, every session whose page read a derivation over that table renders again, so a reader sees another reader's change as it lands.\",\"A test that reaches a database server or a model needs it running, unless it was recorded. The command `crow check --record-fixtures` runs the tests against the real servers and keeps what each statement and each request answered under the `.crow/fixtures` directory.\",\"The command `crow test --fixtures` runs the tests from what was kept, in both modes, and names a statement or a request nothing recorded.\",\"A request's fixture is named by its method, its URL and what it sends, so a changed prompt to a model is a changed request. Its headers, and the key in them, are no part of it.\",\"The module `llm` makes a model a connection. The function `reach` takes a URL: `anthropic://` and a model's name, or `openai://` with a host and a model's name for any server speaking the OpenAI chat completions, such as Ollama or vLLM. The key is read from the environment when a call is made.\",\"A conversation is a list of turns, built a turn at a time: `ask` adds the user's, `said` the model's reply, `told` a tool's result. The function `chat` asks the model and answers a reply with its text, its calls, its usage and why it stopped.\",\"Tools are functions. Writing `tools = [weather]` hands the function, and the compiler writes its schema from the parameters and the comment above it. The program dispatches the model's calls and hands each result back.\",\"The function `embed` answers a matrix of embeddings, a row per text, from an OpenAI-compatible endpoint. A reply can also be followed piece by piece with `follow` and `next`.\",\"A package is a directory of Crow files rooted at itself. The crowfile names each dependency as a git repository and a version, or as a local path, and `crow install` checks each one out into the `crow_modules` directory.\",\"A program imports a package's parts with `use`: the line `use vectors.flat` reads the flat index of the vectors package, and `use retrieval.bm25` the BM25 ranking of the retrieval package.\",\"Every package in the Crow repository is held to a reference. The retrieval package matches scikit-learn and rank_bm25 to the last digit, the vectors package matches faiss's positions exactly, and the optimize package follows scipy step for step.\",\"A page is a view served at an address: `page['/', home]` renders the view for each visit and keeps it live over a socket, so a change to a fact renders again without a reload.\",\"A view is a function that answers elements: a stack of headings, paragraphs, fields and buttons. A field is bound to a fact, and a button names the handler its click runs.\",\"A handler is a transaction. It runs on a working copy of the session's facts, sees its own writes at once, and when it ends its writes land as one. A render never sees a handler half done.\",\"A page and its session: the page request renders the reader's session once, and the page's socket adopts that session, its facts and the render already on screen.\"]}",
